From Product Requirements to Live Guidewire Implementation

Introduction — Turning Requirements into Delivery

Guidewire implementations involve multiple stages, from understanding product specifications and defining business requirements to developing, testing, and validating the final solution.

Traditionally, these activities rely on documents, spreadsheets, manual analysis, and coordination between product owners, developers, and testers. Translating complex product structures and business requirements into implementation-ready specifications can take considerable time, while maintaining consistency and traceability across the delivery lifecycle adds another layer of complexity.

Hexaware’s AI-powered framework addresses this challenge by connecting product definition, rapid prototyping, requirements engineering, AI-assisted development, and testing into a unified delivery process.

Let’s see how this journey works, from the initial product specifications to a working screen in Guidewire PolicyCenter.

Part 1 — From Product Specifications to a Digital Product Model

In a typical Guidewire project, the team receives one or more documents from the client that define the product structure, coverages, and business rules.

For our Swiss Commercial SME Property and Liability product, two key inputs provide the foundation for this process.

The first is the Product Definition Specification, or PDS. It describes the product structure, coverages, and market-specific information required to configure the product.

The second is the rating document, an actuarial artefact also used by underwriters. It defines the rating structure and the calculations required to determine the premium.

Together, these documents provide the context needed to understand the product and its rating logic.

Using AI, we can automatically transform these inputs into a structured product mind map, generated through XMinds.

This mind map captures the product hierarchy and its associated questions, attributes, and coverages, providing a structured representation that can be imported directly into the APD application.

Within APD, the team can explore the product structure, drill down into attributes, and navigate the associated buildings, locations, and coverages.

Once the product structure has been established in APD, it can be imported into Guidewire PolicyCenter.

This creates a more efficient path from product documentation to a structured product configuration.

But defining the product is only one part of the journey. The next challenge is translating business requirements into a form that product owners, developers, and testers can use consistently.

Part 2 — From Requirements to Rapid Prototyping

In a conventional project, product owners typically define features, requirements, and backlogs using documents and spreadsheets.

Hexaware’s approach introduces AI-powered rapid prototyping to make those requirements more tangible.

Instead of relying exclusively on written descriptions, the team can generate an interactive prototype that simulates the relevant PolicyCenter experience in a matter of hours.

The prototype provides a shared reference point for product owners, developers, and testers. It allows the team to review the proposed functionality, understand the intended user experience, and refine requirements before implementation begins.

Let’s look at two examples from the Swiss Commercial SME product.

The first requirement relates to the Policy Information screen.

The standard Guidewire screen includes fields such as SSN and preferred language. For the Swiss product, these need to be replaced with appropriate local alternatives.

The prototype demonstrates the changes, including replacing the SSN field with a Swiss UID identifier using the required format and replacing the preferred language field with a dropdown.

The second requirement relates to the Risk Analysis screen.

The existing prototype contains five tabs. We introduce a sixth tab, called Hazards and Exposure, to bring together relevant peril information from different areas and make it available to support underwriting decisions.

These examples show how the prototype turns business requirements into something the entire team can review and validate.

The product owner and team can iterate on the prototype until the requirements are agreed upon and formally signed off.

Once approved, the prototype becomes the foundation for generating the implementation handoff.

Part 3 — Generating the Development and Testing Handoff

With the prototype agreed upon, the next step is to translate it into a detailed handoff package for the downstream teams.

The framework generates an implementation prompt, feature files, and a detailed implementation specification.

The specification brings together the information needed to implement and validate each requirement, including user stories, business rationale, acceptance criteria, test cases, business rules, dependencies, and requirement coverage.

It also identifies the relevant Guidewire implementation artefacts, such as Gosu and PCF, where applicable.

Each requirement is documented in a structured format, including its expected behavior and the scenarios that need to be validated.

For example, the Natural Hazard Assessment requirement includes acceptance criteria covering successful execution, low-confidence results, timeouts, and other relevant scenarios.

This level of detail helps the team understand not only what needs to be built, but also how the implementation should behave under different conditions.

The completed handoff document is shared with the development and testing teams in parallel.

This allows AI-assisted development and AI-assisted testing to begin from the same agreed specification, rather than relying on separate interpretations of the requirements.

And this is where the development journey begins.

Part 4 — From Specification to Live Screen

For the development phase of our Software Development Life Cycle, Hexaware uses its AI Framework to accelerate delivery on core insurance systems, including policy, billing, and claims.

The framework is designed to combine the speed of AI-assisted code generation with the governance required for enterprise insurance platforms.

AI can generate Gosu, PCF, and test code in minutes. But speed alone is not enough. The implementation must follow the project’s architecture, naming standards, business rules, and security requirements.

To achieve this, the framework follows a governed AI delivery pipeline built around three gates.

The first is the Knowledge Gate, where AI-generated responses are grounded in the project’s own architecture and implementation patterns through the Guidewire Developer Assistant.

The second is the Policy Gate, where documented standards, naming conventions, bundle practices, and personally identifiable information rules are translated into checkable requirements.

The third is the Human Review Gate. Before code reaches a trusted branch, it must be reviewed and approved by a qualified reviewer.

Together, these controls help ensure that AI accelerates development without removing the governance and accountability required for enterprise delivery.

Let’s see how this works in our Zurich CH Commercial SME Property and Liability implementation on Guidewire PolicyCenter.

The project brings together six user stories, forty-one acceptance criteria, and sixty-nine test cases, with full requirement coverage across all six stories.

The implementation is traceable back to the original specification and the handoff documents created earlier in the process.

The journey begins with the complete specification, which defines the user stories and acceptance criteria for the Zurich Commercial SME product.

The specification also establishes how requirements should be interpreted. Developers and AI coding assistants are expected to review each user story in full, and any unconfirmed information must be explicitly identified rather than treated as fact.

This provides a common foundation for implementation and review.

From the specification, we generate a structured implementation prompt for the Hexaware AI Assistant.

For the Natural Hazard Assessment capability, the prompt defines the objective and directs the assistant to follow existing PolicyCenter design patterns.

Importantly, the process does not begin with immediate code generation. The assistant must first investigate the existing implementation, including the PolicyLocation design, APD integration model, and established extension patterns.

This discovery step helps ensure that the proposed implementation fits the existing system rather than introducing an unrelated design.

The findings are then captured in the Main System Design Document, which connects the relevant requirements to the platform version, development branch, author, and source specification.

For this demonstration, we focus on REQ-005: Natural Hazard Assessment Integration.

The objective is to introduce an asynchronous integration that retrieves high-confidence peril data from an external hazard service and populates the Natural Hazard Zone information.

From the main design, we move to the requirement-specific System Design Document for REQ-005.

This document defines the business requirements, acceptance criteria, entities, fields, and integration pattern needed for the implementation.

It provides a detailed architectural record that can be reviewed and approved before code is generated.

Once the design is established, the framework moves into implementation.

Here, we can see the generated Gosu entity, ZHazardAssessment_Ext.

The entity includes a foreign key to PolicyLocation, typekeys for status and the derived hazard zone, and fields for confidence, flood, hail, storm, and landslide scores.

The fields are named, typed, and documented according to the project’s established conventions, reflecting the guidance supplied through the Knowledge Gate.

But generated code must still be validated before it becomes trusted implementation.

The next step is verification in Guidewire Studio.

The entity is opened directly in the development environment, where the reviewer can inspect its properties and confirm the foreign key to PolicyLocation, the RiskAssessmentResult implementation, the typekeys, and the underlying columns.

This provides a transparent way to validate that the generated implementation aligns with the approved design.

The same principles apply to the user interface.

A new Hazard and Exposure panel set is built using the existing Risk Analysis card pattern. It brings together the location, canton, status, confidence, and individual peril scores, along with an action to assess selected locations.

The result is a user interface that follows familiar PolicyCenter patterns while introducing the required functionality.

Now we can see the implementation in the live PolicyCenter application.

An underwriter opens a submission and navigates to Risk Analysis. The new Hazards and Exposure tab appears alongside the existing sections, including UW Issues, Contingencies, and Prior Losses.

The underwriter can assess a location and view the resulting flood, hail, storm, and landslide scores, together with the confidence rating returned by the integration.

What began as a product requirement has now progressed through specification, prototyping, handoff, design, code generation, and verification to a working screen in Guidewire PolicyCenter.

Part 5 — What This Approach Changes

Bringing these stages together changes how teams collaborate across the delivery lifecycle.

Product specifications and rating documents provide the foundation for building a structured product model.

AI-powered prototyping turns business requirements into a tangible experience that product owners can review and approve.

The generated handoff then gives developers and testers a common, detailed reference, including user stories, acceptance criteria, implementation guidance, and test cases.

Finally, the governed AI development process uses that specification to guide design and code generation, while preserving validation, human review, and traceability.

The result is a more connected delivery process, where requirements are not isolated in documents, prototypes are not disconnected from implementation, and generated code is not accepted without verification.

For a program such as Zurich CH Commercial SME, this approach supports faster delivery, consistent implementation patterns, and clearer traceability from business requirements to working functionality.

It also helps teams address the complexity of localized insurance products, including Swiss UID identifiers, market-specific requirements, and natural hazard assessment, without compromising the controls needed for enterprise delivery.

Closing — From Business Intent to Governed Implementation

This is the value of Hexaware’s AI-powered delivery approach for Guidewire.

